(a) (i)    Candidates are still struggling to spell the term correctly.
           Answer:
           Nitrogen fixing bacteria or Rhizobium bacteria;
 (ii) Most of the candidates were able to state the correct answer.
           Answer:
           Legumes or an example of legume mentioned;
 (iii) Many candidates could not identify the type of symbiotic relationship.  Some could correctly
           give the description.

           Answer:
           Name:  Mutualism;
           Description:  Both organisms benefit from this relationship;
 (iv) Candidates were able to score two marks on the fixation and absorption.  Because the focus was
           on the activities in the root nodule, candidates could not score a mark for stating the role played
           by the bacteria in soil fertility and they referred to the legume plant dying to improve soil fertility instead.
           Answer:
           Bacteria make use of nitrogen gas from the soil air to synthesise body protein;
           The bacteria, in the nodules convert nitrogen gas or N2 to ammonia NH4+;
           The plant roots can absorb/use NH4+;
           Bacteria get carbohydrates from the plant roots;
           Proteins in dead bacteria are converted to ammonium compounds and nitrates;

(b) (i)    Candidates could not score maximum marks because they could not specify that it is the
           movement of water and not liquid in general, as well as candidates not mentioning soil pores
           but rather writing “in a narrow tube”.
           Answer:
           The upward movement of water; with dissolved (mineral) salts from the soil water table or through the
           micro-pores; leading to salt accumulating on the surface;
 (ii) Most candidates could identity the conditions and score maximum marks.
           Answer:
           Occurs naturally from minerals in the rocks, parent material or ground water;
           Solutes in irrigation water;
           low rainfall conditions;
           High evaporation rate, in very hot conditions;
           Poor drainage;
           Salt deposition;
           Excessive fertiliser application;
 (iii) Most candidates could score maximum marks.
           Answer:
           Bare patches occur in cultivated fields, kill plants, toxic to plants, lower plant productivity;
           Soil surface becomes powdery, leads to poor soil structure, soil becomes erodible, sodic soils are
           formed
           High salt concentration causes plasmolysis, wilting, exosmosis or reduced water uptake
 (iv) The term, salt crust was an obstacle to candidates to give correct answers to this question as it
           was confused with the impact on soil salinity.
           Answer:
           Devise ways to remove excess salt from irrigation with brackish water;
           Heavier irrigation with longer intervals provided drainage is adequate;
           Irrigation of soil originating from sedimentary rocks must be done carefully;

                 Dams or irrigation canals must be cemented;
                 Good quality irrigation water needed;
                 Keep the drainage system in good working condition;
                 Plant brack-resistant crops, plant salt tolerant plants such as grasses, shrubs or trees;
                 Apply Calcium sulfate or Ca2SO4 fertiliser;
                 Minimise long fallows and crop rotation;
                 Field rinse or flush with large volumes of water with low salt concentration;

