9 (a) Candidates   confused   the   features   of   wind   pollinated   flowers   and   insect   pollinated   flowers   and   thus
             could not score marks.
             Answer:
             No special odours, scent or nectar as they do not have to attract insects;
             Small as there is no need to attract insects;
             Most have no petals or dull petals;
             Stamens and stigmas exposed to air currents, dangling or protruding outside;
             Large amount of pollen to make up for wastage in air/to increase chances of pollination;
             Pollen smooth, light, easily airborne;
             Stigma feathery to catch pollen from wind;

      (b)    The question was well answered by most candidates.  Candidates lost marks for not specifying which
             aspect they were referring to.  Candidates only listed correct answers without specifying if it is for poor
             seed quality or low temperature.
             Answer

             Poor seed quality
             Seed deterioration leads to loss of viability/poor or no germination;
             Results in less vigorous crops;
             they produce weaker seedlings;
             leading to reduced crop yield;

             Low temperature:
             Low temperature delays germination or crop maturity; restricts root expansion/growth; reduce
             photosynthesis/uptake of water and mineral salts; leading to low production/reduced yield;
             Periodic low temperature reduces excessive transpiration, which improves crop yield;

      (c)    Most candidates scored maximum marks on this question.  However, they lost marks for not correctly
             stating the answers.  They just stated mitosis is diploid instead of it produces haploid cells.
             Answer:
               Mitosis                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Meiosis
               Produces two daughter cells Produces                            Produces four daughter cells; Produces
               diploid (2n) cells Produces somatic cells                       haploid (n) cells; Produces gametes;
               Cells produced are genetically identical;                       Cells produced are genetically different;
               Gives no variation                                              Allows variation;
               Used for asexual reproduction                                   Used for sexual reproduction;

      (d)    Many candidates could score maximum marks, but some lost marks for not specifying again if the
             roles are for auxin or gibberellins.
             Answer:

             Gibberellins:
             Initiate seed germination;
             Promotes stem growth between the nodes of the stem by cell elongation;
             Promote root growth;
             Initiate flowering;
             Promote an increase in fruit size;

           Auxins:
           Control the direction in which the shoots and roots grow in relation to light and gravity;
           Promote stem growth by elongation;
           Inhibit the growth of side shoots near the tip of the main stem;
           Promote root branching and fruit development
           Promote cell division;

10 (a) This question was not well answered as candidates could not link the roles of the manager to the
           livestock enterprise.
           Answer:
           It involves supervising farm workers;
           Consulting with animal nutritionists and feed representatives to develop feeding plans or rations for optimum
           production;
           Working with veterinarians to ensure herd health management such as vaccinations or treatment against
           pest and diseases;
           Developing reproduction such as breeding programmes to improve breed characteristics and productivity;
           Sourcing and purchasing farming and milking machines as well as ensuring maintenance thereof,
           purchasing or maintaining farm equipment;
           Deciding how additional resources may be obtained;
           Planning production and marketing programmes;
           Record keeping;
           Recruitment of farm workers

     (b)   Most candidates could not describe the challenges correctly.
           Answer:
           Climate variability such as drought and floods;
           High input costs;
           Disease outbreaks;
           Unreliable local supply of inputs;
           Limited access to credit;
           Access to the market;
           Skills or labour turnover;

     (c)   Candidates were mainly referring to incentives instead of what a subsidy is.  Candidates could not
           describe how the farmers benefit from the example of a subsidy.
           Answer
           A subsidy is an amount of money that is provided by the government; so that prices of commodities are
           affordable to consumers or farmers;
           For example, the government may instruct seed producers to sell seeds at a lower price that is affordable for
           subsistence farmers;
           Farmers get the seeds at a cheaper price; the producer gets the desired price for the seeds as the difference
           in the price is paid by the government;

     (d)   This question was not well answered as some candidates were referring to challenges instead of
           attitude change and mobility.  Also, candidates listed the points without specifying if the points were
           for attitude change or mobility.
